<div class="page">
  <p>Complementing his creation of the new electronic journal, <span data-field="journal_name">{{ journal_name }}</span>, Charles Prebish has assembled on this panel prominent scholars in the newly-emergent field of Buddhist ethics. In their papers they investigate several strands of Theravāda and Mahāyāna ethical reflection. By bringing philological tools to bear on key texts and analyzing modes of ethical argumentation, they extend their inquiry beyond descriptive ethics to the level of meta-ethics, and thereby provide fertile ground for the work of other Buddhists and ethicists in general.</p>
  <p>Dan Cozart’s paper, “Cutting the Roots of Virtue,” Tsongkhapa on the Results of Anger,” examines Tsongkhapa’s writings on anger in relation to earlier Mahāyāna Buddhist texts. Cozart broaches the possibility that Buddhist views of anger as a “root affliction” (<span data-field="term_anger">{{ term_anger }}</span>) that “cuts the roots of virtue” force the conclusion that angry people may be unable to achieve liberation. To Tsongkhapa, one of the main problems with the emotion of anger is the ascription of autonomy to the object of anger. This reification or hypostatization of the object of anger entails the angry one in his or her own mental constructs and resultant suffering. However, when the notion that a mere moment of anger can cause the loss of aeons of virtue is juxtaposed with the doctrine of <span data-field="term_virtue">{{ term_virtue }}</span>, certain questions arise. For example, might Tsongkhapa’s tradition itself be succumbing to reification—of an emotion, rather than the object thereof—in its attempt to critique anger?</p>
  <p>One might also wonder whether the negative valuation of anger is only in response to the unenlightened substantialization of the object of one’s anger. Cozart outlines how the consequences of anger are disastrous if the object of the anger is a bodhisattva. Presumably, a bodhisattva is an individual who has stayed in the realm of <span data-field="realm_bodhisattva">{{ realm_bodhisattva }}</span> in order to take on the suffering of sentient beings and kindly lead them to liberation. If anger is directed at a bodhisattva, the consequences of that anger are not only for the angry one but also for the bodhisattva, who is bound to the cycle of suffering. This is a significant point, as it suggests that the ethical framework being examined is not merely concerned with the individual’s path to liberation, but also with the ethical implications of one’s actions toward those who are engaged in the practice of compassion.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <p>Granted, given the status of bodhisattvas in Mahāyāna Buddhism, anger toward them might be seen as contrary, for example, to precepts against defaming the Three Jewels. Even allowing for textual and philosophical bases for this continual of anger toward a bodhisattva, data about the institutional and historical contexts of the formulation of Mahāyāna prohibitions against anger might prove illuminating. Perhaps the issue of anger toward high-ranking Buddhist figures such as bodhisattvas says more about the political organization of conflict in the Sangha than about the religious status of these figures.</p>
  <p>Cozart also cites a contemporary Gelugpa scholar who maintains that anger will indeed have a disastrous effect on the roots of virtue, as argued 600 years earlier by Tsongkhapa. Although this claim may make sense in terms of a scholarly reading of the texts, it is not clear whether the claim is meant to be a universal ethical principle or merely a reflection of the specific doctrinal concerns of the Gelugpa tradition. The Gelugpa tradition, in this view, places a high value on the cultivation of compassion, and anger is seen as a profound impediment to this goal. The tension here is between a universal ethical claim and a tradition-specific doctrinal concern. If the claim is universal, it must be supported by evidence that transcends the specific doctrinal framework of the Gelugpa school. If it is tradition-specific, then its application to other Buddhist traditions must be approached with caution.</p>
  <p>The question of whether anger is universally detrimental to virtue is complex. Some traditions emphasize the cultivation of positive states of mind, such as loving-kindness, as the primary ethical goal. In these traditions, anger is seen as a hindrance to the development of these positive states. Other traditions, however, focus more on the cessation of suffering, and anger is viewed as a source of suffering in itself. The relationship between these two approaches—cultivating positive states versus eliminating negative ones—is a central theme in Buddhist ethics. The debate over anger highlights the diversity of ethical approaches within Buddhism.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <p>The debate over anger also touches upon the relationship between ethics and metaphysics. Is anger merely a psychological state, or does it have ontological implications for the nature of reality? If anger is seen as a mere psychological state, then its impact is primarily on the individual's mental well-being. If, however, anger is seen as a fundamental aspect of the nature of reality, then its impact extends to the structure of existence itself. Buddhist philosophy often navigates this terrain, suggesting that suffering, which is closely related to anger, is a fundamental characteristic of conditioned existence.</p>
  <p>Furthermore, the discussion of anger raises questions about the role of intention in moral evaluation. In many ethical systems, the intention behind an action is considered as important as the action itself. In Buddhism, the concept of karma emphasizes the role of intention in shaping future experiences. Anger, therefore, is not just an emotion, but an intentional state that carries karmic weight. This perspective suggests that the ethical significance of anger is not merely about its immediate psychological effects, but about its potential to shape one's entire trajectory of experience.</p>
  <p>The concept of "cutting the roots of virtue" suggests a causal relationship between anger and the erosion of moral qualities. This implies a teleological view of ethical development, where the goal is the cultivation of virtue. The challenge, as noted, is whether this causal link is a universal truth or a specific doctrinal assertion. If it is universal, it must be demonstrable across diverse Buddhist traditions. If it is specific, then its claims must be understood within the context of the tradition that posits it.</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <p>One possibility that must be entertained here is that there is something common to the particular cases that individuals agree constitute “impureness,” but people at that time in South Asia could not agree on what it was or give the commonality an adequate articulation (through an inductive process of reasoning). In his paper, Hallisey seems to allow for the possibility of commonality (and perhaps principles or criteria), when he states that in the context of ethical particularism, we “develop a sense of judgment” and “some general truths are evident.” This suggests that while specific instances may differ, there might be underlying principles that guide ethical judgment. This approach avoids the pitfalls of both universalism and particularism by seeking a middle ground.</p>
  <p>But if in fact “auspiciousness” does simply refer to an agreed-upon cluster of actions without any demonstrable commonality or principles linking them, one must ask whether we are dealing with ethics or with mere convention. In other words, at what point does ethical particularism become something other than ethics? Or is a definition of “ethics” that excludes convention overly narrow?</p>
  <p>In his analysis of key Mahāyāna Buddhist texts, David Chappell highlights for us the fact that the ways Buddhists classify and group texts do not necessarily correspond with how actual Buddhists and their religious communities draw from those texts to meet various ethical and philosophical needs. Chappell also highlights different notions of skill-ful means (<span data-field="term_skillful_means">{{ term_skillful_means }}</span>) and compassion (<span data-field="term_compassion">{{ term_compassion }}</span>). His discussion causes me to wonder about the basis, if any, on which one might be compelled to accept the notion that <span data-field="term_upaya">{{ term_upaya }}</span> is always a morally good thing. Is it always a good thing, or is it context-dependent? If it is context-dependent, then the notion of <span data-field="term_upaya">{{ term_upaya }}</span> becomes less of a universal principle and more of a pragmatic tool.</p>
  <p>In Mahāyāna Buddhism, might there not be an element of what Kierkegaard referred to as a “teleological suspension of the ethical,” especially when <span data-field="term_action">{{ term_action }}</span> takes the form of actions that violate certain precepts or Buddhist values? (One extreme example of this is the Chan teacher Ch'i-ji (J. Gutei) supposedly inducing enlightenment by cutting off the finger of an acolyte who had imitated him.)</p>
</div>

